WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.396
"Benefits and Side Effects of the Pneumonia Vaccine"

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.240
Vaccines are considered

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.720
one of the greatest
public health achievements

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:12.720
of the last century,

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.160
having eradicated smallpox,

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.920
a scourge that killed
hundreds of millions of people,

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.080
and greatly reducing
other major diseases,

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.120
such as measles and polio.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.320
To this day,
vaccines are estimated

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:29.400
to save millions of lives
a year.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.920
More than 90 percent
of U.S. children

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.920
get common
childhood vaccinations,

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.360
such as polio or their measles,
mumps, and rubella shot,

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.640
but most adults fail
to get their full complement

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.920
of recommended
adult vaccinations.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.960
Assuming you got
all your childhood vaccinations,

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.480
and aside from
any emergent pandemic needs,

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.640
the CDC recommends
all healthy adults

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.600
get annual flu shots,

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.560
tetanus boosters
every ten years,

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.000
though the World Health
Organization

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.960
doesn't think
this is necessary,

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.000
shingles vaccination
at age 50,

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.800
and pneumonia vaccine
at age 65.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.200
Certain groups need others,

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.040
such as a hepatitis B series
for healthcare workers

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.120
or men who have sex with men.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.360
Ask your medical professional
for a personalized schedule.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.240
How safe are vaccines?

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.440
In a systematic review
and meta-analysis,

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:24.600
the RAND Corporation screened
more than 50,000 citations

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.400
and concluded
that routine vaccinations

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.720
can be considered safe,

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.480
with only rare
serious adverse effects,

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.240
such as severe
allergic reactions,

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.080
in 1 to 10 in a million,

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.920
and transient autoimmune
syndromes Guillain-Barré

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.320
and immune
thrombocytopenic purpura

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.680
in 1 to 3 in a million,

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.040
and 10 to 30 in a million

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.920
for flu shots and MMR vaccines,
respectively.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.720
Of course, any adverse effects
should be weighed against

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.360
any protective effects
that vaccines may provide.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.240
In my last video,

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:58.040
I talked about the effectiveness
of flu vaccines.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.440
What about pneumonia vaccines?

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.600
"Pneumonia may well be called
the friend of the aged,"

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.360
wrote the "Father
of Modern Medicine,"

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.880
Sir William Osler,

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.040
over a hundred years ago
in 1898.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.320
"Taken off by it
in an acute, short,

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:14.760
not often painful illness,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.400
the old man escapes those
'cold gradations of decay'

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.800
so distressing to himself
and to his friends."

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.920
The thought
was that pneumonia

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.360
mercifully killed those
who would soon die anyway

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:29.440
from potentially a more
protracted, painful illness.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:30.400
But, these days,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.680
healthy older adults
hospitalized for pneumonia

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.080
are not significantly
more likely to die

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.280
in the subsequent two years

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.480
than younger adults
in the same situation.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.480
Because of comorbidities
at older ages, though,

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.520
pneumonia is the fourth leading
cause of death in the world

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.240
and the ninth leading cause
in the United States.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:52.720
The most common cause of
community-acquired pneumonia,

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:54.680
as opposed to hospital-acquired,

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.200
is a bacteria
known as pneumococcus,

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.560
or Streptococcus pneumoniae.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:00.960
In addition to pneumonia,

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.880
pneumococcus can cause
inner ear infections,

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.920
sinusitis, or pinkeye.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.480
It gets serious when it starts
to invade the bloodstream,

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.600
which can result in meningitis,
infection of the brain;

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.000
endocarditis,
infection of the heart valves;

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.080
or sepsis, a life-threatening
organ dysfunction

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.040
caused by blood poisoning.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.960
Thankfully, we have vaccines
against pneumococcus.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.400
The first was developed
over a century ago,

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.280
but they fell out of favor
after penicillin was discovered,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.400
thinking antibiotics
would eliminate the threat.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.160
Unfortunately,
these days up to 40 percent

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.080
of these kind
of streptococcal infections

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.320
are resistant
to at least one antibiotic,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.000
and despite our miracle drugs,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.120
mortality rates
of invasive pneumococcus

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.440
in the elderly remain
around 15 to 30 percent.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.400
However,
randomized controlled trials

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.600
have found
that pneumococcus vaccines

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.720
reduce the risk
of those 65 and older

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.640
getting pneumococcal pneumonia
by 64 percent,

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.000
and, even more importantly,

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.240
your risk of invasive
pneumococcal disease

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.360
by 73 percent.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:04.920
Like the flu vaccine,

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.360
population studies have found
that pneumonia vaccines

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.760
can reduce the risk
of both heart attacks

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.200
and the overall risk of dying,
but unlike flu vaccines,

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.240
there aren't randomized
controlled trials

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:29.600
to confirm
these bonus benefits.

